Production, 2026-09-04, World of Tanks (live lane, reuse-only): three
campaigns arrived within two seconds. executeReuse served the first by
reusing all 18 warm accounts. For the other two `mine` came out 0 — every
account spoken for by the first — and the step STILL wrote ACTIVE rows with
plannedAccounts 0 and targetAccounts 0. Legacy's backfillActiveTasks read
both as "under target" (targetAccounts || plannedAccounts || 0 falls through
to the market floor, 18) and topped each up with 18 FRESH pool accounts. 36
fresh accounts on a reuse-only game, deployed, not recoverable. WoT demoted
to shadow.

The rule this fixes into place: any row shape the lane writes must be one
legacy already produces. Legacy's fleet sweeps read status "active" and act
on it — for lane rows too, by design — so an invented shape is acted on by
code that never expected it. Legacy's own inline reuse writes the same empty
row but immediately tops it up through executeTask, whose reuse-only branch
refuses fresh accounts; the lane has no such top-up, so the row stood.

Now, when `mine` is empty, executeReuse touches no host and records what
legacy records for "nothing to spend": skip_reuse_only on a reuse-only game
(the exact shape executeTask's reuse-only branch writes), skip_no_accounts
otherwise. Both are RETRYABLE; the lane re-decides every cycle and reuses the
moment an account frees up. Dry-run is unchanged (legacy's dry-run reuse also
records planned with 0).

Five tests drive the REAL non-dry-run executeReuse with startContainer and
readRegistry stubbed: three campaigns together give one active task, two
skip_reuse_only rows, one bot restart, and zero rows that are ACTIVE with no
accounts — the predicate backfill would have matched; a non-reuse-only game
gets skip_no_accounts; the skip carries the decision snapshot; a later cycle
reuses once accounts free; dry-run is pinned unchanged.

Doc §11 records the incident, the rule, and an audit of every row shape the
lane writes against it.

On the separate backfill leak: backfillActiveTasks in this checkout already
passes recycledOnly: true for reuse-only games — bb0419e, 2026-08-21, in the
deployed branch's history — and every reported leak (08-24, 08-30, 09-04)
postdates it. Either prod's copy lacks that hunk or isReuseOnlyGame(task.game)
is false for those rows on prod. Not a new fix; the existing hunk is packaged
standalone for a prod-side check. Declined on those grounds, not on risk.

Suite 703/704 vs 698/699; the single failure is unchanged.
